index 00000000..ed8b712a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/applied-reload.md
@@ -0,0 +1,96 @@
+# Atomic hot reload and applied status
+
+`--autoreload` supports changes to plugins, locations, upstreams and the
+`locations` list of an existing server. Each candidate is validated and all
+request providers are prepared before one atomic publication. Every request
+captures the complete generation when its context is created and uses it for
+routing, plugins, upstream selection and retries. Host and weight changes rebuild
+server indexes even when the server's location names have not changed.
+
+A failed candidate keeps the complete previous request graph and the previous
+`current_config`. Unknown plugin categories, plugins compiled out of the binary,
+missing plugin references and plugin constructor failures are errors. `-t` rejects
+these errors as well. Built-in plugin names and `pingap:admin` are reserved.
+
+The memory cache backend is a process-wide singleton. Changing the already registered memory backend capacity/mode requires restart;
+request-level settings on an existing memory cache plugin remain reloadable.
+
+Changes to basic settings, certificates, storage or server settings other than
+`locations`, and adding/removing servers, require a restart. `--autoreload`
+rejects the whole candidate with category `unsupported`; it does not apply a
+supported subset. `--autorestart` records `restart_required` and requests the
+existing graceful restart while the previous graph continues serving.
+
+## Read-only admin API
+
+`GET /api/apply-status` uses the same timestamped admin authorization as the
+existing admin APIs. A custom admin mount prefixes the path. Other methods return
+405, and unauthorized calls return 401. The endpoint does not trigger a reload.
+Existing `/api/basic` and configuration APIs remain available.
+
+```json
+{
+  "schema_version": 1,
+  "process_id": 1234,
+  "process_instance_id": "019b0aaa-bbbb-7ccc-8ddd-eeeeeeeeeeee",
+  "applied": {
+    "attempt_id": 7,
+    "operation_id": "00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001",
+    "config_hash": "AB12CD34",
+    "config_digest": "64 lowercase hexadecimal SHA256 characters",
+    "applied_at_unix_ms": 123456789
+  },
+  "last_attempt": {
+    "attempt_id": 8,
+    "operation_id": "00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002",
+    "config_hash": "CD34AB12",
+    "config_digest": "64 lowercase hexadecimal SHA256 characters",
+    "state": "failed",
+    "failure": { "category": "plugins", "message": "constructor failed" }
+  }
+}
+```
+
+`applied` and `last_attempt` are nullable before initialization. An attempt's state
+is `preparing`, `applied`, `failed` or `restart_required`. Its failure is nullable;
+categories include `validation`, `storage`, `plugins`, `locations`, `upstreams`,
+`server_locations` and `unsupported`. A document that cannot be parsed has null
+operation/hash/digest fields. `applied` is only updated in the same atomic swap as
+the complete request graph. Preparing and failed attempts retain the previous
+applied identity. Obsolete attempt completions cannot publish or overwrite a
+newer attempt. IDs increase within each process, including failed attempts.
+
+`process_id` is the serving process's actual PID, including daemon mode.
+`process_instance_id` is a fresh UUID, renewed if a daemon fork changes PID, so
+PID reuse does not reuse an identity. A caller confirming an owned publication must match its
+retained child PID, process instance, operation, full digest and attempt state.
+
+`config_hash` is the existing CRC32 configuration description hash and is retained
+for compatibility. `config_digest` is SHA256 over the complete resolved
+configuration serialized as canonical JSON:
+
+```rust
+let mut value = serde_json::to_value(&resolved_config)?;
+value.sort_all_objects();
+let canonical = serde_json::to_vec(&value)?;
+let digest = hex::encode(sha2::Sha256::digest(&canonical));
+```
+
+Object keys are explicitly sorted recursively, including when `preserve_order` is enabled, arrays
+retain their order, and no formatting whitespace is added. The digest includes
+all serialized fields, including null defaults, certificate data and storage
+secrets; it does not use the masked CRC32 descriptions. `PingapConfig::digest()`
+implements this contract. The paired TOML/JSON/SHA256 fixture is in
+`pingap-config/tests/fixtures/applied-config.*`.
+
+## Operation marker
+
+An orchestrator can place a UUID in the `data` field of a mock plugin named
+`rcoder:publication`; this existing configuration shape supplies `operation_id`.
+Use an exact, highest-priority `/_pub/<UUID>` location on each existing listener,
+referencing only this mock plugin, to return the UUID in both the response body
+and `X-Rcoder-Publication`. No new configuration field is introduced. Confirm the
+marker through actual HTTP requests on every listener in addition to checking
+admin applied status; graph publication alone does not prove listener reachability.
+
+`pingap --apply-protocol-version` prints `1` and exits without loading configuration. Older binaries that lack this query must be rejected before changing business services.
